{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba skills/mission/SKILL.md | sed -n '180,245p'\"","aggregated_output":"   180\t- **What it hides** -- the complexity behind the interface that callers don't need to know about\n   181\t- **Why this boundary** -- what makes this the right place to draw the line\n   182\t\n   183\tIf a module has a large interface relative to its implementation, ask whether it should be split differently or whether some of its interface belongs in a higher-level module. A good test: can a worker implement this module in a single task and a validator test it at the boundary? If not, the module may be too shallow or too broad.\n   184\t\n   185\t#### Existing Codebases\n   186\t\n   187\tWhen the mission modifies an existing codebase (not greenfield), the design must evaluate the current code structure against the planned architecture. For each module the design touches, read the existing code and assess whether it aligns with the target design. If existing modules are shallow, tightly coupled, or conflict with the planned architecture, the design should call this out explicitly and recommend refactoring as part of the implementation. These refactoring decisions flow naturally into task ordering in Phase 4.4 -- refactoring tasks go before the feature tasks that depend on them.\n   188\t\n   189\tPresent it to the human with a recommendation.\n   190\t\n   191\tBefore presenting it, run the Phase 4.3 design gate:\n   192\t- Verify `design.md` contains all five required section headings from `templates/design-template.md`: Architecture Decision, Module Inventory, Type Catalog, Error Strategy, and Integration Points. If any are missing, regenerate `design.md` before human review.\n   193\t- For greenfield missions or missions with explicit architecture changes, verify the Architecture Decision section contains 2-3 scored architecture candidates using the template rubric.\n   194\t- For existing codebase missions without architecture changes, scoring is not required. Verify the Architecture Decision section documents the current architecture and explains how the planned changes fit.\n   195\t- Enforce the hard floor: no recommended candidate can score 0 on any scoring dimension. If a candidate scores 0 on any dimension, flag it and recommend against it.\n   196\t- If candidates tie on total score, present the tied candidates to the human for discussion and selection. Do not apply an arbitrary tie-breaker.\n   197\t\n   198\tAfter the human approves design.md, populate 5 of the 6 Deterministic Anchors in the plan: workspace layout, primary domain types, error handling strategy, test strategy, and chosen architecture. Source values from design.md's Architecture Decision, Type Catalog, and Error Strategy sections. Each anchor is a one-liner pointer with a source reference. The sixth anchor (task decomposition strategy) is populated in Phase 4.3a after the human approves the task strategy.\n   199\t\n   200\tIf approved, proceed to Phase 4.3a.\n   201\t\n   202\t### Phase 4.3a: Task Strategy Approval\n   203\t\n   204\tBefore generating tasks.md, present a task decomposition strategy for human approval:\n   205\t\n   206\t1. **Decomposition approach:** layered (types and domain logic first, then transport/CLI), vertical (each task cuts end-to-end through all layers), or hybrid (foundation layer first, then vertical slices). State which and why.\n   207\t2. **Target task count range:** e.g., \"6-8 feature tasks + 4-5 closing tasks.\" Justify the range based on the module inventory from design.md.\n   208\t3. **Dependency ordering rationale:** which tasks must come first and why (e.g., \"Task 1 defines core types that all subsequent tasks import\").\n   209\t\n   210\tThe human approves this strategy before tasks.md is generated. If rejected, revise and re-present.\n   211\t\n   212\tAfter approval, populate the task decomposition strategy anchor in the plan's Deterministic Anchors section.\n   213\t\n   214\tIf approved, proceed to Phase 4.4.\n   215\t\n   216\t### Phase 4.4: Generate and Review Tasks\n   217\t\n   218\tGenerate `tasks.md` (ordered checklist of implementation tasks).\n   219\t\n   220\t#### Task Quality Standard\n   221\t\n   222\tEach task must produce a concrete, testable output. When the approved strategy (Phase 4.3a) is vertical, tasks cross all layers. When the strategy is layered or hybrid, tasks may be scoped to a single layer, provided each task still has a clear done state and a test surface.\n   223\t\n   224\t**One task = one worker = one commit = one validation cycle.**\n   225\t\n   226\tA task is good if you can demo it and verify it independently after it lands.\n   227\t\n   228\t#### Task Topology\n   229\t\n   230\tEach task in tasks.md must include topology metadata in this fixed format:\n   231\t\n   232\t```markdown\n   233\t- [ ] Task N: <name>\n   234\t  Depends on: <prior task numbers, or \"none\">\n   235\t  Consumes: <surfaces from prior tasks -- types, interfaces, endpoints>\n   236\t  Produces: <surfaces this task creates -- types, modules, interfaces, endpoints>\n   237\t  Layers: <logical architectural layers touched, e.g. domain, transport, storage>\n   238\t```\n   239\t\n   240\tLayers are logical groupings of modules (domain, transport, storage, configuration, etc.) defined in the design narrative, not columns from the Module Inventory table.\n   241\t\n   242\tWhen the approved strategy (Phase 4.3a) is layered or hybrid and a task's Layers field lists more than 2 architectural layers, flag the task for human review with a recommendation to split or approve as-is. When the approved strategy is vertical, the layer count flag does not apply since vertical tasks are expected to cross all layers.\n   243\t\n   244\tWhen a task adds public surface area to a module (new exports, API endpoints, CLI flags), include the implementation behind that surface in the same task. Do not split a module's interface from its implementation across tasks -- the per-task validator judges depth from a single diff and will flag an interface without the work behind it.\n   245\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
