{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba README.md | sed -n '75,105p'\"","aggregated_output":"    75\t- **Proposal** -- what you're building and why\n    76\t- **Specs** -- detailed requirements written as WHEN/THEN rules (e.g. \"WHEN a user submits the login form with wrong credentials, THEN show an error message\")\n    77\t- **Design** -- architecture decisions and technical approach. The design evaluates 2-3 architecture candidates against a four-dimension rubric (separation of concerns, testability, task independence, module depth) and picks the highest-scoring option. A mandatory gate verifies the design includes an architecture decision, module inventory, type catalog, error strategy, and integration points.\n    78\t- **Tasks** -- an ordered checklist of implementation work. Before generating tasks, the orchestrator presents a decomposition strategy (layered, vertical, or hybrid) with a target task count for your approval. Each task includes topology metadata -- which prior tasks it depends on, what it consumes, and what it produces.\n    79\t\n    80\tEvery tasks.md ends with a set of **mandatory closing tasks** that run after the feature work: a smoke test (one end-to-end test through the primary user flow against real dependencies), a cleanup task (remove dead code, unused deps, and leftover scaffolding), a module shape re-check (catch drift between design.md and what the code actually exposes -- required once a mission has 5+ feature tasks), a documentation task, and a CLAUDE.md + ARCH.md generation task (creates project convention files if they don't already exist). A CI configuration task is optional and added when relevant.\n    81\t\n    82\tAfter the tasks are defined, the orchestrator runs a **coverage audit**. This maps every requirement from the PRD to one or more tasks. If anything is missing, it flags it. If a requirement was intentionally changed or dropped, you approve that decision explicitly. For substitutions, the orchestrator also checks that the replacement approach is fully covered by existing tasks -- if the substitution drops something important, it's flagged as a gap. Nothing falls through the cracks by accident.\n    83\t\n    84\tContract assertions trace back to specific spec capability and scenario identifiers for end-to-end traceability.\n    85\t\n    86\tFinally, it writes a **contract** -- a checklist of things the finished code should do, written so they can be tested from the outside (by running the code, not by reading the source). For example: \"the login endpoint returns a 401 when given wrong credentials.\" Each item traces back to a PRD requirement and is tagged with how it should be verified (by executing the code, checking its output, or inspecting the source). Before moving on, the orchestrator runs a testability check to catch any vague or untestable items early. You review the contract, confirm which AI models handle each role (see [Configuration](#configuration)), and approve launch.\n    87\t\n    88\t### Stage 2: The orchestrator builds and validates autonomously\n    89\t\n    90\tFor each task, the orchestrator sends a worker (Codex or Claude, your choice) a brief describing what to build. The worker writes the code, commits its changes, and writes a handoff report. Workers never see the contract -- this is intentional, and explained in [information walls](#information-walls) below.\n    91\t\n    92\tAfter the first task passes validation, the orchestrator runs a foundation audit -- checking that the committed code's module structure and type names match the design. This catches naming drift before it cascades into subsequent tasks.\n    93\t\n    94\tAfter each worker finishes, two validators check the work:\n    95\t\n    96\t1. **Scrutiny validator** -- a thorough quality check that runs a sequence of checks:\n    97\t   - Do tests exist? (auto-fails the task if not)\n    98\t   - Does the project build, do the tests pass, does the linter pass?\n    99\t   - Code review and code standards check\n   100\t   - Module depth review -- did the change make modules shallower or leak internal details?\n   101\t   - Complexity check -- is the code more complex than it needs to be?\n   102\t   - Security review (only when the changes touch auth, secrets, user input, network boundaries, or filesystem trust boundaries)\n   103\t   - Contract check -- does the code do what the contract says it should?\n   104\t   - Execution check -- actually runs the code to confirm it works, rather than just reading the source\n   105\t   - Test health checks -- silent skip detection, diff coverage on changed lines, and test quality review\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBDT2VWX50X67ACA3GT71T3","ts":"2026-04-29T01:32:59Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba skills/mission/templates/design-template.md | sed -n '1,90p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Design Template\n     2\t\n     3\tUse this template for every `design.md`. Include all five mandatory sections below.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Architecture Decision\n     6\t\n     7\tFor greenfield missions or missions with explicit architecture changes, evaluate 2-3 architecture candidates. Score each candidate on every dimension from 0-3, then recommend one candidate.\n     8\t\n     9\tFor existing codebase missions without architecture changes, document the current architecture and explain how the planned changes fit. Scored candidates are not required.\n    10\t\n    11\t| Candidate | Separation of concerns | Testability | Task independence | Module depth | Total | Recommendation |\n    12\t|-----------|------------------------|-------------|-------------------|--------------|-------|----------------|\n    13\t| Candidate A | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-12 | Recommended / Not recommended |\n    14\t| Candidate B | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-12 | Recommended / Not recommended |\n    15\t\n    16\t### Scoring Rubric\n    17\t\n    18\t#### Separation of Concerns\n    19\t\n    20\t| Score | Criteria |\n    21\t|-------|----------|\n    22\t| 3 | Each responsibility maps to exactly one unit. No unit mixes domain logic with transport, storage, or configuration. |\n    23\t| 2 | Mostly isolated but 1-2 units mix concerns pragmatically. |\n    24\t| 1 | Several units mix concerns. Domain logic tangled with transport or storage in more than one place. |\n    25\t| 0 | No clear separation. Business logic, I/O, and configuration interleaved throughout. |\n    26\t\n    27\t#### Testability\n    28\t\n    29\t| Score | Criteria |\n    30\t|-------|----------|\n    31\t| 3 | Each module testable in isolation with swappable dependencies via interfaces or dependency injection. |\n    32\t| 2 | Most modules testable in isolation. One or two require a real external dependency. |\n    33\t| 1 | Tests require significant setup or multiple running services. Modules tightly coupled to dependencies. |\n    34\t| 0 | No practical way to test modules in isolation. Testing requires the full system running. |\n    35\t\n    36\t#### Task Independence\n    37\t\n    38\t| Score | Criteria |\n    39\t|-------|----------|\n    40\t| 3 | Separately buildable or deployable units. A task can modify one unit without touching others. |\n    41\t| 2 | Explicit interface boundaries within a single build unit with enforced contracts. |\n    42\t| 1 | Directory/namespace separation only. No enforcement beyond developer discipline. |\n    43\t| 0 | Flat structure. No meaningful boundaries. Every task touches shared state. |\n    44\t\n    45\t#### Module Depth\n    46\t\n    47\t| Score | Criteria |\n    48\t|-------|----------|\n    49\t| 3 | Modules expose small public API relative to implementation. Callers don't need to understand internals. |\n    50\t| 2 | Reasonable interface-to-implementation ratio. One or two modules expose more surface than they hide. |\n    51\t| 1 | Several modules are thin wrappers. Public API nearly as large as implementation. |\n    52\t| 0 | No meaningful abstraction. Public surface and implementation effectively the same. |\n    53\t\n    54\tAfter the table, include a short rationale for the recommended architecture. If candidates tie on total score, present the tied candidates and their tradeoffs for human selection.\n    55\t\n    56\t## Module Inventory\n    57\t\n    58\tList each planned module or major component. Use this table format:\n    59\t\n    60\t| Module name | Interface width | What it hides | Why this boundary |\n    61\t|-------------|-----------------|---------------|-------------------|\n    62\t| `<module>` | Narrow / moderate / broad, with the public entry points named | Implementation details callers do not need to know | Reason this responsibility belongs behind this boundary |\n    63\t\n    64\t## Type Catalog\n    65\t\n    66\tList domain types only. Type names must come from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` when the term exists there. Do not include field-level detail.\n    67\t\n    68\t| Type name | Purpose | Owning module |\n    69\t|-----------|---------|---------------|\n    70\t| `<type>` | One-line purpose | `<module>` |\n    71\t\n    72\t## Error Strategy\n    73\t\n    74\tDefine:\n    75\t- Error type hierarchy\n    76\t- Propagation rules\n    77\t- Which layer throws each error category\n    78\t- Which layer catches and translates each error category\n    79\t\n    80\tKeep this at the design level. Do not list every possible error instance unless the specs require it.\n    81\t\n    82\t## Integration Points\n    83\t\n    84\tDescribe how modules call each other. Use this table format:\n    85\t\n    86\t| Caller | Callee | Method/interface | Direction |\n    87\t|--------|--------|------------------|-----------|\n    88\t| `<module>` | `<module>` | Function, command, event, adapter, or interface name | Inbound, outbound, or internal |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
