{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba skills/mission/SKILL.md | sed -n '279,385p'\"","aggregated_output":"   279\t## Phase 4a: Spec Coverage Audit\n   280\t\n   281\t> **Rules:** 5\n   282\t> **Goal:** Verify every PRD requirement maps to at least one task before writing the contract.\n   283\t> **Inputs:** PRD (read from GitHub issue URL in plan), specs/, tasks.md\n   284\t> **Outputs:** coverage-audit.md, updated specs/tasks if gaps found\n   285\t\n   286\tBefore writing the contract, verify that every PRD requirement is covered by at least one task.\n   287\t\n   288\t### Steps\n   289\t\n   290\t1. **Read the PRD** from the GitHub issue created in Phase 3.\n   291\t2. **Read tasks.md** and all spec files from `specs/`.\n   292\t3. **Build a coverage table** mapping every PRD requirement to the task(s) that implement it:\n   293\t\n   294\tUse the format in [templates/coverage-audit.md](templates/coverage-audit.md).\n   295\t\n   296\tThe \"Prerequisites met?\" column applies to Substitutions only. For Interpretations and Removals, write \"--\".\n   297\t\n   298\t4. **Flag gaps and classify deviations.** For each requirement that is not directly covered:\n   299\t  **If GAP (no plan to implement):** Add task (and spec requirement if missing) / Defer (out of scope, track for later) / Skip (intentionally not implementing, document why).\n   300\t\n   301\t  **If DEVIATION (implementing differently than spec), classify:**\n   302\t    **Interpretation** (spec is ambiguous, reasonable reading) -- batch-approve with COVERED items.\n   303\t    **Substitution** (different approach) -- explicit human approval per item. State the trade-off: \"The spec says X. We are doing Y instead because Z. This means [concrete consequence].\"\n   304\t    **Removal** (not implementing) -- explicit human approval per item. State impact: \"Users who need X will not be able to use the tool for that purpose.\"\n   305\t\n   306\t  #### Prerequisite check for substitutions\n   307\t  For every Substitution, ask: \"Does the deviated approach require implementation work that the mapped task will not cover?\" Break down the deviated approach into its prerequisites. If any prerequisite is not covered by the mapped task or another existing task, route it through gap remediation (Add task / Defer / Skip). The deviation cannot be approved until all prerequisites are resolved. Resolution options:\n   308\t    **Add a task** to cover the missing prerequisite (triggers spec/task re-review)\n   309\t    **Expand the mapped task** to include the prerequisite (update tasks.md, re-present for review)\n   310\t    **Reclassify as Removal** (forces the human to acknowledge the requirement is being dropped, not substituted)\n   311\t\n   312\t  Mark approved deviations as **APPROVED-DEVIATION** in the coverage table (not COVERED). This status persists through the contract and is re-surfaced during the spec delta check (Phase 9).\n   313\t\n   314\t  **Present substitutions and removals in a dedicated review step.** The human approves or rejects each individually. Do not mix them with the COVERED items review. Interpretations may be batch-approved with the main table.\n   315\t\n   316\t5. **Present the coverage table and gap recommendations** to the human. Follow the standard review pattern: AI presents, AI recommends, human decides.\n   317\t\n   318\t6. **If specs change** (new spec requirements added to cover gaps), re-present the affected spec files for human review before proceeding. The human must approve any new or modified specs.\n   319\t\n   320\t7. **If tasks change** (new tasks added to cover gaps), re-present the updated tasks.md for review. The human must approve the final task list before proceeding.\n   321\t\n   322\t8. **Persist the coverage audit to disk.** Write the coverage table to `openspec/changes/<change-name>/coverage-audit.md`. This file is needed for mission resume (so a resumed session can see what was already mapped without re-running the audit) and for contract writing (Phase 5 cross-references it). For each Substitution deviation, include the prerequisite analysis (which prerequisites were checked, which were covered, which were gaps, and how they were resolved). This ensures a resumed session knows why a substitution was approved safe.\n   323\t\n   324\t9. **Record the coverage audit** in the plan. Update the Spec Coverage Audit section with the PRD issue URL, gap count, and resolutions.\n   325\t\n   326\t**Gate -- proceed when:**\n   327\t- [ ] Coverage audit complete -- every PRD requirement mapped\n   328\t- [ ] All gaps resolved (added, deferred, or skipped)\n   329\t- [ ] All deviations classified and approved by human\n   330\t- [ ] Changed specs/tasks re-approved by human (if any changed)\n   331\t- [ ] coverage-audit.md persisted to disk\n   332\t- [ ] Plan updated with audit results\n   333\t\n   334\t---\n   335\t\n   336\t## Phase 5: Write Contract\n   337\t\n   338\t> **Rules:** 2, 5\n   339\t> **Goal:** Write the validation contract that workers never see.\n   340\t> **Inputs:** coverage-audit.md, specs/, tasks.md, PRD (read from GitHub issue URL in plan)\n   341\t> **Outputs:** contract.md (human-approved, testability pre-flight passed)\n   342\t\n   343\tCreate `contract.md` in the change directory (`openspec/changes/<change-name>/contract.md`).\n   344\t\n   345\t### Contract Rules\n   346\t\n   347\t1. Group assertions by task, matching the task list in tasks.md.\n   348\t2. Assertions should describe **observable behavior** verifiable from outside the code (HTTP response, CLI output, file on disk, UI state, build passing, etc.). Where this is genuinely not possible, use `[inspection]` assertions -- see the verification method tags in the contract template for caps.\n   349\t3. Do NOT reference implementation details (function names, variable names, internal data structures).\n   350\t4. Be specific and unambiguous. A validator reading only the contract and the diff should be able to check every assertion without guessing.\n   351\t5. Workers never see this file.\n   352\t6. **Write the minimum set of assertions needed to verify the task is complete and correct.** Each assertion should trace to a specific spec capability/scenario identifier, including error behaviors specified in the PRD or specs. Do not add assertions for edge cases, nice-to-haves, or defensive behaviors unless the PRD explicitly requires them. If an assertion would not cause you to reject the task when it fails, do not include it. Place assertions on the earliest task where they become verifiable -- if a behavior spans multiple tasks, put the assertion on the task where it can first be checked, not where the work begins.\n   353\t\n   354\t### Cross-Referencing Specs\n   355\t\n   356\tFor each task, use the coverage audit from Phase 4a to find which spec capability/scenario identifiers map to it. Write assertions that capture the specific details from those scenarios -- not generic behavioral checks, but assertions that would fail if the specific scenario were not implemented. Each assertion must trace back to a specific spec capability and scenario number.\n   357\t\n   358\t**Process for each task:**\n   359\t1. Look up the spec capability/scenario identifiers mapped to this task in the coverage table.\n   360\t2. Read the corresponding spec requirements (WHEN/THEN scenarios) for precise behavioral expectations.\n   361\t3. Write assertions that verify those specific behaviors with specific values, endpoints, messages, or states from the PRD and specs.\n   362\t4. If the source PRD or spec mentions specific constraints (limits, formats, error messages, status codes), those constraints must appear in the assertions verbatim.\n   363\t\n   364\t### Contract Template\n   365\t\n   366\tEach assertion must include a **verification method** tag indicating how a validator should check it:\n   367\t- `[execution]` -- verifiable by running a command, HTTP request, or test script (strongest)\n   368\t- `[output]` -- verifiable by checking command output, file contents, or API response\n   369\t- `[inspection]` -- verifiable only by reading code (weakest)\n   370\t\n   371\tCap `[inspection]` assertions at **20% of total per task**. If more than 20% require inspection, rewrite them as `[execution]` or `[output]` where possible. If an assertion genuinely cannot be made executable, it is acceptable -- but flag it during contract review.\n   372\t\n   373\tUse the format in [templates/contract.md](templates/contract.md).\n   374\t\n   375\tReference spec coverage with a `<!-- Specs: capability/N, capability/M -->` comment, where `capability` is the spec folder name and `N` is the scenario number within that spec file.\n   376\t\n   377\tWhen a spec scenario traces to an APPROVED-DEVIATION item in the coverage audit, mark it with `(DEVIATED)` in the Specs comment. The assertion itself describes the actual implemented behavior (the deviation), not the original spec requirement. This gives the human visibility during contract review into which assertions exist because of an approved deviation.\n   378\t\n   379\t### Contract Review\n   380\t\n   381\tAfter writing the contract, present it to the human following the standard review pattern: AI presents, AI recommends, human decides.\n   382\t\n   383\tFor each task section, highlight:\n   384\t- Which spec capability/scenario identifiers the assertions trace to\n   385\t- Whether the assertion count feels right (too many suggests over-specification; too few suggests gaps)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
