{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md | sed -n '145,158p'\"","aggregated_output":"   145\t> **Orchestrator:** \"The **Design Template** now requires **Architecture Candidate Scoring**. I evaluate 2-3 options on four dimensions -- separation of concerns, testability, **Task Independence**, and module depth -- each scored 0-3. No candidate can score 0 on any dimension. If two tie, you pick.\"\n   146\t>\n   147\t> **Dev:** \"What stops the design from using different type names each run?\"\n   148\t>\n   149\t> **Orchestrator:** \"The **Type Catalog** in design.md names every domain type using terms from the ubiquitous language. The **Foundation Audit** checks after task 1 that the committed code actually uses those names. If it drifts, I generate a corrective task before task 2 starts.\"\n   150\t>\n   151\t> **Dev:** \"And if context compacts mid-mission and you lose the design details?\"\n   152\t>\n   153\t> **Orchestrator:** \"The **Deterministic Anchors** in the plan survive compaction. They're one-liner pointers to the six key decisions -- workspace layout, task strategy, domain types, error handling, test strategy, and chosen architecture. I can re-read design.md if I need the full detail, but the anchors tell me what to look for.\"\n   154\t\n   155\t## Example dialogue (test health)\n   156\t\n   157\t> **Dev:** \"The benchmark shows irontodo-v2 has 97 tests but only 49 actually run. What happened?\"\n   158\t>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
