{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' openspec/changes/deterministic-planning/mission/validations/adversarial-a.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Adversarial Review A\n\nBase SHA: `01139a17d0a23e4e15d49cafa9b7286e6dc8261d`\n\n## Commit Inventory\n\n- `29bc8ac` [task-1.1] Add design template, config rules, and architecture scoring gate\n- `c152c84` [task-2.1] Update contract template and Phase 5 to use spec references\n- `ab5d9f9` [task-3.1] Add deterministic anchors to plan template and Phase 4.3\n- `0195f82` [task-4.1] Add Phase 4.3a, task topology, and strategy-aware quality standard\n- `303aa20` [task-5.1] Add CLAUDE.md template and closing task\n- `796084a` [task-6.1] Add foundation audit after task 1 in Phase 8\n- `1fb7edf` [task-10.1] Update documentation for deterministic planning changes\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical\n\n1. **Spec traceability cannot be generated deterministically from the coverage audit.**\n\n   Phase 5 now requires each contract assertion to trace to specific `capability/scenario` identifiers and says to look those identifiers up in the coverage table (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:356`, `skills/mission/SKILL.md:359`). But Phase 4a still builds a table mapping PRD requirements to tasks, and the coverage template has only `#`, `PRD Requirement`, `Spec Requirement`, `Task(s)`, and `Status` columns (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:292`, `skills/mission/templates/coverage-audit.md:4`). It stores WHEN/THEN text, not the spec folder name or scenario number. The contract template can pass a grep for `Specs:`, but the orchestrator has no reliable source for `add-todo/1` style identifiers and may invent IDs or reuse row numbers.\n\n   Fix: add a required `Spec Reference(s)` column to `coverage-audit.md` using `capability/N` values, update Phase 4a to populate it from spec file paths and scenario order, and make Phase 5 consume that column.\n\n### Warnings\n\n1. **Foundation audit depends on parsing free-text `Produces`, so it can miss drift or flag valid tasks incorrectly.**\n\n   Phase 4.4 defines `Produces` as a free-text list of \"types, modules, interfaces, endpoints\" (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:236`). The foundation audit later says to extract module names and type names from that same field (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:700`, `skills/mission/SKILL.md:702`). There is no syntax that distinguishes `modules` from `types`, so names like `User`, `UserService`, `POST /users`, and `Repository` are ambiguous. The audit also adds a stricter check that matching directories or files must exist for module names (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:701`), even though the Module Inventory describes design boundaries, not necessarily filesystem names.\n\n   Fix: make `Produces` structured, for example `modules=[...] types=[...] interfaces=[...] endpoints=[...]`, and have the audit compare only the relevant buckets against Module Inventory and Type Catalog.\n\n2. **Deterministic anchors are not actually included in worker briefs.**\n\n   The plan template says every worker brief references the Deterministic Anchors (`skills/mission/templates/plan.md:31`). But Phase 8 Step 1 only says to read `templates/worker-brief.md` (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:511`, `skills/mission/SKILL.md:515`), and the worker brief template has no Deterministic Anchors section (`skills/mission/templates/worker-brief.md:6`, `skills/mission/templates/worker-brief.md:9`). That means the anchors may survive in `plan.md`, but workers do not see them unless the orchestrator remembers to add them ad hoc.\n\n   Fix: add a `## Deterministic Anchors` section to `worker-brief.md` and instruct Phase 8 Step 1 to copy the six anchor lines from the plan into every brief.\n\n3. **The `Test strategy` anchor has no required source in `design.md`.**\n\n   Phase 4.3 says to populate five anchors from `design.md` after approval, including `test strategy`, but then only names Architecture Decision, Type Catalog, and Error Strategy as source sections (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:198`). The design template has no Test Strategy section (`skills/mission/templates/design-template.md:5`, `skills/mission/templates/design-template.md:56`, `skills/mission/templates/design-template.md:64`, `skills/mission/templates/design-template.md:72`, `skills/mission/templates/design-template.md:82`). The plan placeholder points vaguely to `design.md` (`skills/mission/templates/plan.md:37`). This invites the orchestrator to make up the test strategy anchor or source it from inconsistent places.\n\n   Fix: either add a mandatory Test Strategy section to the design template and config rules, or change the anchor to source from Phase 4.4 tasks and make that timing explicit.\n\n4. **README documentation now conflicts with the mission skill in several places.**\n\n   The README says design evaluates 2-3 candidates and picks the highest-scoring option (`README.md:77`), but SKILL allows existing-codebase missions without architecture changes to skip scoring and requires human selection for ties (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:193`, `skills/mission/SKILL.md:196`). The README lists closing tasks with documentation before CLAUDE.md + ARCH.md generation (`README.md:80`), while SKILL requires CLAUDE.md + ARCH.md before documentation (`skills/mission/SKILL.md:258`, `skills/mission/SKILL.md:260`). The README also still says each contract item traces to a PRD requirement and labels `contract.md` as \"traced to PRD\" (`README.md:86`, `README.md:162`), conflicting with the new spec-reference model.\n\n   Fix: update README wording to mirror the actual branching rules, closing task order, and spec-reference traceability.\n\n### Nits\n\n1. **`Vertical slice` is now defined as the whole strategy-aware task standard.**\n\n   `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` defines `Vertical slice` as the default task quality standard and then includes layered and hybrid behavior inside that definition (`UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md:20`). That muddies the term: a vertical slice should mean a task that crosses layers, while the broader concept is the approved task quality standard. This is minor, but it can reintroduce the same terminology confusion Task 4.1 was trying to remove.\n\n   Fix: keep `Vertical slice` narrow, and add or use a separate term such as `Task Quality Standard` for the strategy-aware rule.\n\n## Cross-Task Pass\n\n- Phase 4.3a feeds Phase 4.4 correctly for strategy approval and the >2 layers flag.\n- The foundation audit is positioned after task 1 passes validation, but it needs structured topology metadata to be reliable.\n- Deterministic anchors exist in the plan, but they are not wired into worker briefs, and one anchor lacks a required source.\n- The closing task order is correct in SKILL.md but inconsistent in README.md.\n- Spec traceability is only partially migrated: contract output uses `Specs:`, but coverage audit still lacks the identifiers needed to produce those comments.\n\n## Summary\n\nSeverity counts: 1 critical, 4 warnings, 1 nit. Shipping recommendation: do not ship until the critical coverage-audit/spec-reference gap is fixed. The warnings should be addressed before relying on deterministic planning in real missions, especially the free-text `Produces` parsing and missing anchor propagation to worker briefs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
